<Base title="Electroporation & quantitative molecular transport" active="Research" description="Molecular dynamics and diffusion modeling connect electropore formation to transport across permeabilized membranes."><article>
<header class="container page-intro"><a class="back" href={sitePath("/research/#transport")}>← Research</a><p class="eyebrow">Membrane biophysics · 2025</p><h1>From a molecular pore<br/><span>to measurable transport.</span></h1><p class="lead">Connecting molecular dynamics with diffusion modeling to study movement across a permeabilized membrane.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s10867-025-09682-w" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read the paper ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="#figures">Explore the figures ↓</a></div></header>
<div class="container project-layout"><aside><p class="eyebrow">In this study</p><a href="#question">Research question</a><a href="#approach">Across scales</a><a href="#findings">Molecular transport</a><a href="#figures">Figure gallery</a><p class="aside-note">Journal of Biological Physics<br/>51, article 18 (2025)</p></aside><div class="article-body">
<section id="question"><h2>How does a pore become a pathway?</h2><p>Opening a membrane pore is only part of the delivery process. This work connects the molecular events of electroporation with the transport of molecules through the resulting opening.</p></section>
<section id="approach"><h2>Connecting two scales.</h2><p>Molecular dynamics follows pore formation, including lipid headgroup reorientation and hydration. Pore dimensions then inform a finite-difference diffusion model.</p><Figure source="transport" src="transport-figure-2.webp" alt="Six side-view molecular snapshots of pore formation from 0 to 500 picoseconds" caption="Figure 2. Molecular snapshots during electropore formation."/></section>
<section id="findings"><p class="eyebrow">Central finding</p><h2>Molecular size shapes transport.</h2><p>The model compares glucose, calcein, and bleomycin. The study reports faster diffusion for smaller molecules, illustrating how molecular properties influence transport through a pore.</p><Figure source="transport" src="transport-figure-5.webp" alt="Time-dependent transport and concentration plots for glucose, calcein and bleomycin" caption="Figure 5. Quantitative transport and concentration profiles."/></section>
<section id="figures"><h2>From structure to transport.</h2><p>Explore the remaining figures from the study.</p><div class="figure-grid">
